      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 The Australasian College of Dermatologists.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is a potentially life-threatening dermatological disease involving large areas of skin loss with systemic symptoms. This study evaluated the efficacy of air-fluidised bed therapy for TEN patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Of 27 people with TEN, 11 used air-fluidised beds (the air-fluidised group) and 16 used standard beds (the control group). Days to complete re-epithelialisation, re-epithelialisation rate, incidence of complications, mortality, pain measured by visual analogue score and the incidence of cutaneous infection were compared in these groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The mean body surface area of involvement was 77.0\u2009±\u200911.8% and baseline mean severity-of-illness score for TEN (SCORTEN) was 2.81\u2009±\u20091.08. The re-epithelialisation rate in the air-fluidised group was 100% but was only 56.3% in the control group (P\u2009<\u20090.05). There was a significant difference in the time taken to complete re-epithelialisation between the air-fluidised group (13 days [95%\u2009CI: 9.0-17.0]) and the control group (21 days [16.5-25.5], P\u2009<\u20090.05). Furthermore, the incidence of complications was 18% in the air-fluidised group versus 75% in the control group, including fewer cutaneous infections (P\u2009<\u20090.05). There was a significant reduction in pain among the air-fluidised group compared with the control group (P\u2009<\u20090.05). There were no deaths in the air-fluidised group while 19% of the control group died."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Air-fluidised beds can reduce the time to complete re-epithelialisation, relieve pain and increase the re-epithelialisation rate of TEN patients, but there was no significant difference between them in mortality rate in our study."
          }
        ]
      },
